Wrote `ADR-001-notification-architecture.md` (85 lines). Definitive recommendation: **Redis Streams** on a dedicated instance, with the Postgres transactional outbox providing billing durability.

The decision turns on operational complexity: a 6-person team with no infra engineer, no Kafka experience, and a 2-week budget cannot absorb a 3-broker Kafka cluster's standing tax (KRaft/ZooKeeper, partitioning, rebalancing, lag monitoring, upgrades), while Redis Streams extends infrastructure they already run. The throughput case is lopsided — 10x growth caps around ~10k events/s versus ~100k+ ops/s on one Redis instance — and Kafka's exactly-once (KIP-98) still requires the same consumer-side idempotency we'd build on Redis, so nothing is forfeited on the billing guarantee.

All six required sections are present in order; every named property (throughput, ordering, retention, consumer groups, exactly-once, operational complexity) is addressed for both options, with Kafka's cons enumerated in Alternatives Considered and the honest trade-offs (single-writer ceiling, RAM-bound retention, no native DLQ) listed in Consequences.
